{"name":"Watery Sea","hex":"#88bfe7","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"watery","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Resembling or characteristic of water.","Wet, soggy or soaked with water."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/watery"},{"term":"sea","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Semai."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A large body of salt water.","The ocean; the continuous body of salt water covering a majority of the Earth's surface."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sea"}],"entries":[{"title":"Water","blocks":[{"text":"Water is an inorganic compound with the chemical formula H2O. It is a transparent, tasteless, odorless, and nearly colorless chemical substance. It is the main constituent of Earth's streams, lakes, and oceans. Water is also the fluid of all known living organisms, in which it acts as a solvent. Water, being a polar molecule, undergoes strong intermolecular hydrogen bonding which is a large contributor to its physical and chemical properties. It is vital for all known forms of life, despite not providing food energy or being an organic micronutrient. Due to its presence in all organisms, its chemical stability, its worldwide abundance, and its strong polarity relative to its small molecular size, water is often referred to as the \"universal solvent\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/H2O_2D_labelled.svg/330px-H2O_2D_labelled.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Water"},{"title":"Sea","blocks":[{"text":"A sea is a very large body of salty water. The term has two distinct meanings. The first is the interconnected body of seawater that spans most of Earth, known as the sea or the ocean. The second is a named body of water: usually either a marginal sea, or a large, nearly landlocked body of water."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Mediterranean_Sea_16.61811E_38.99124N.jpg/330px-Mediterranean_Sea_16.61811E_38.99124N.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sea"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Water-3D-balls-A.png/500px-Water-3D-balls-A.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Water-3D-balls-A.png/500px-Water-3D-balls-A.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Ball-and-stick model of a water molecule","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWater-3D-balls-A.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Water_drop_001.jpg/500px-Water_drop_001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Water_drop_001.jpg/1280px-Water_drop_001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Water_drop_001.jpg/500px-Water_drop_001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A drop of water falling toward water in a glass","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWater_drop_001.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Pillow_basalt_crop_l.jpg/500px-Pillow_basalt_crop_l.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Pillow_basalt_crop_l.jpg/1280px-Pillow_basalt_crop_l.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Pillow_basalt_crop_l.jpg/500px-Pillow_basalt_crop_l.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"This pillow basalt on the seafloor near Hawaii was formed when magma extruded underwater. Other, much older pillow basalt formations provide evidence for large bodies of water long ago in Earth's history.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APillow_basalt_crop_l.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Water_density_volume_mass_metric.png/500px-Water_density_volume_mass_metric.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Water_density_volume_mass_metric.png/1280px-Water_density_volume_mass_metric.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Water_density_volume_mass_metric.png/500px-Water_density_volume_mass_metric.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Ways of presenting the density of water using five metric units of length, volume, and mass.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWater_density_volume_mass_metric.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Tetrahedral_Structure_of_Water.png/330px-Tetrahedral_Structure_of_Water.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Tetrahedral_Structure_of_Water.png/330px-Tetrahedral_Structure_of_Water.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Tetrahedral structure of water","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATetrahedral_Structure_of_Water.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Heat_capacity_of_water_2.jpg/500px-Heat_capacity_of_water_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Heat_capacity_of_water_2.jpg/500px-Heat_capacity_of_water_2.jpg?utm_source=en.w